s.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`; those are the re-enrollment validation CAs. That list is optional because the enrollment CA is always trusted for re-enrollment, and each settings block consults only its own `validation_cas`. CAs listed only in the initial block's `validation_cas` do not extend re-enrollment acceptance; during a CA rotation, add the old issuance CA to the re-enrollment block's list. The re-enrollment validator compares the presented TLS leaf against those CAs; the re-enrollment block's `chain_level_validation` and `allow_expired` values are stored but not consulted, and expiry is governed by `enable_expired_renewal`."],[1441,"For devices to authenticate to AWS IoT Core with certificates issued by a Lamassu CA, that CA must first be registered in the AWS account. The connector handles this registration automatically in response to the `EventCreateCAKey`, `EventImportCAKey` and `EventUpdateCAMetadataKey` events, but the operation **only fires when the administrator explicitly requests it** through the CA metadata field."],[606,"For each role, separate \"view\" from \"change\", limit resources through grants, relations or attributes and avoid `*` unless the role must automatically cover future actions."],[561,"For entity permissions, Lamassu combines permissions with OR logic: it is enough that one policy of one of the principals allows the action. Listings contain the union of the resources visible to all of them."],[666,"For example, `function (event) { return event.data.status == \"NO_IDENTITY\"; }` limits a subscription to devices without an identity."],[569,"For example, this rule allows reading active certificates issued by a specific CA:"],[2515,"For Gateway, sign-in, pods, dependencies and messaging, see Deployment troubleshooting. For device enrollment, see EST diagnostics."],[914,"For initial mTLS, `client_certificate_settings` contains `validation_cas`, `allow_expired` and `chain_level_validation`. The inspected flow receives one certificate credential; it does not guarantee forwarding every handshake intermediate to the validator. For a direct case, configure the bootstrap’s direct issuer as validation CA and test the actual Gateway path."],[2350,"For intermediates, add `-verify_other intermediates.pem`. Require successful response verification and inspect the status corresponding to `device.crt`, issuer, serial, `thisUpdate` and `nextUpdate`. `Response verify OK` validates the response only: also observe `good`, `revoked` or `unknown`. Do not accept an identity based only on the command’s exit code."],[1213,"For logs, networking, pods or Gateway headers, continue in Deployment troubleshooting. For identities issued, revoked or bound partially, use Renewal and recovery and Devices and identities."],[746,"For metrics, traces and technical logs of the deployment, see Troubleshooting. Audit answers \"who tried to change what\"; observability explains how the system behaved."],[409,"For PKI, check the certificate, the chain, OCSP or CRL with the same type of client production uses."],[91,"For production, use Helm so database, identity, credentials, certificates, storage and upgrades remain explicit."],[1055,"For the administrator"],[2508,"For the device to request its certificate, prepare the DMS and EST client."],[1466,"For the firmware developer, `auto` mode is transparent: the device performs its EST enrollment as usual and, as a side effect, it is provisioned in AWS IoT Core with no additional step."],[1056,"For the integrator"],[1471,"For the JITP flow to work correctly:"],[281,"For the recommended design:"],[563,"For this reason, adding a matching principal can only broaden access. Before creating overlapping rules, review the full set of policies a real identity will receive."],[2534,"For unexpected certificate fields, review `Honor Subject`, `Honor Key Usage`, `Honor Extended Key Usages` and `Honor Extensions`. The latter preserves SAN only, without validating entitlement to those names. Validity is not automatically clamped to CA expiration."],[1266,"Forced disconnect on revocation"],[2337,"Format"],[111,"free external ports 80 and 443 on a single-node K3s host"],[820,"From **No Identity**, use **Assign Identity**:"],[1272,"From a protocol standpoint, the device authenticates to AWS IoT Core via mutual TLS (mTLS): it presents its Lamassu-issued X.509 certificate as the client certificate, and AWS IoT Core verifies the certificate chain up to the CA registered in the account. Lamassu acts as the certification authority (CA); the connector ensures that CA is registered in AWS and that the device's certificate is known to the platform before the device attempts to connect (in `auto` mode) or on its first connection (in JITP mode)."],[764,"From onboarding to operation"],[2112,"From the interface"],[2241,"From this view you also reach the most common actions:"],[304,"Front-door SNI routing to internal Gateways"],[581,"full and read-only access to certificates and authorities;"],[583,"full and read-only access to Device Manager, DMS Manager, Validation Authority and alerts;"],[582,"full and read-only access to the KMS;"],[83,"Gateway address selection"],[364,"Gateway API and Envoy CRDs, the Envoy Gateway controller, a `GatewayClass`, a load-balancer implementation and the external network path"],[146,"Gateway HTTP listener port"],[143,"Gateway HTTPS listener port"],[2558,"Gateway token validation removed"],[2621,"Gateway token validation removed"],[1074,"Gateway, TLS, provider and service configuration belongs to Deployment. Identity use in AWS or other destinations belongs to Integrations."],[740,"Generate a controlled mutation in a test environment and check that:"],[2207,"Generate a CSR"],[1621,"Generate a new key and CSR with the selected identifier:"],[1785,"Generate a new key in the planned engine, create the CA and assign it an explicit profile. Do not reuse the operational identifier to hide that it is a different genera