arts with them"],[2619,"High: without a bootstrap principal there is no administrator"],[2594,"Historical device events move from the `devices.events` column to a dedicated `device_events` store with their own endpoints. The events endpoint can also stream events over Server-Sent Events (SSE); to fan events out across service instances, each Device Manager instance binds its own durable AMQP queue."],[1685,"History"],[634,"How it works"],[761,"How Lamassu works"],[475,"How policies express access"],[333,"How the deployment fits together"],[2313,"How the responder works in Lamassu"],[471,"How the two paths relate"],[2366,"How to check the result"],[1256,"How to request registration"],[1201,"HTTP 200 without a device connection"],[1185,"HTTP 500 with an `err` body on enroll or reenroll"],[2315,"HTTP endpoints"],[562,"HTTP routes with constraints are stricter. The same principal contributing the policy must also satisfy the attributes required by the route. Lamassu does not combine one principal's policy with an attribute belonging to another."],[479,"HTTP rules"],[571,"HTTP rules do not point directly at tables. They reference a route schema and grant its logical actions. Job Manager's policies are an example: they distinguish reading, creating, updating and deleting workflows or jobs."],[519,"HTTP rules primarily answer &#x2A;*\"can this identity call this API operation?\"**. On their own they do not produce filters over the external service's database. To limit a route to a specific device, tenant or client, either the schema declares a constraint relating a request value to a normalized attribute of the principal, or the policy pins the request value to a literal with `param_constraints`. The first scopes each identity to its own resources through a single grant; the second restricts a grant to a fixed value regardless of who holds it."],[1356,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's CA service."],[1352,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's Device Manager."],[1348,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's DMS Manager service."],[398,"I cannot sign in"],[1280,"IAM action"],[1362,"IAM actions"],[1634,"ID created automatically and correct DMS."],[892,"ID of the signing CA."],[1088,"ID, owning DMS, certificate CN and bound serial."],[1998,"Identify a certificate by issuer and serial; a name can correspond to several renewals. Check which certificate the consumer actually uses and which occupies the device’s active identity. Identity history and the certificate inventory answer different questions."],[2048,"Identify DMSs, devices and services that trust that chain."],[2432,"Identify the certificate"],[1874,"Identify the certificates, devices and consumers that depend on the CA. Prepare a replacement authority and chain when necessary."],[861,"Identify the fleet"],[702,"Identify the operation"],[359,"Identity"],[1956,"Identity"],[2067,"Identity flow"],[449,"If `amqp.tls` is enabled, verify the TLS side as well:"],[1494,"If `shadow_config.shadow_name` holds a value, the connector uses a Named Shadow with that name; otherwise it uses the Classic Shadow (the Thing's default shadow)."],[1692,"If an expiration or renewal state appears, check certificate dates and DMS thresholds in Devices and identities."],[1691,"If assignment fails, review permissions and operation diagnostics before repeating it. Query the device and its history again to find the persisted result."],[2422,"If creation fails, check permissions, engine availability and the selected algorithm."],[1623,"If curl fails, use Troubleshooting with the saved body. Do not convert it or repeat the request without querying inventory: a timeout or error can follow persisted issuance."],[316,"If data planes and TLS termination must stay independent behind one IP, add a front-door SNI passthrough layer."],[314,"If Gateways need independent policy and you can allocate IPs, use one VIP per Gateway."],[2142,"If generation fails (for example, because of a role without a CA or an invalid signer), the job logs the warning `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ski>` and retries on the next cycle. There is no other renewal path until the cause is fixed, and the last published list keeps being served until it expires. All of this depends on working jobs, storage and signing."],[227,"If Keycloak is external and already has its own reachable URL, point the OIDC settings at it and omit the `/auth` entry from `gateway.extraRouting`."],[419,"If login succeeds but the APIs return 403:"],[1920,"If neither is supplied"],[543,"If no combination of principal, policy and rule allows the operation, Lamassu denies it, unless the HTTP schema declares `skip_authz` on the route or `default_action: allow` on a base path that covers it (see *Authentication-only routes and unmapped paths*). Additionally, `auth.externalAuthorization.failOpen` is `false` by default: if `authz` is unavailable, the Gateway blocks protected routes."],[1690,"If no eligible certificate appears, check that its `Common Name` matches the `Device ID` exactly, it is active and it was issued by the expected CA."],[317,"If none of those are possible, use different external ports or change the external network design."],[313,"If one Gateway can own TLS and routing, use the chart default."],[315,"If only the Kubernetes resources need separation, evaluate merged-Gateway mode and make every listener tuple unique."],[2423,"If OpenSSL cannot read the file, copy the complete PEM block again."],[2424,"If signature or date checks fail, check that you downloaded this CA's certificate and that your computer's clock is correct."],[2729,"If the deployment runs on SQLite, `couch_db` or `dynamo_db`, move it to PostgreSQL first."],[2746,"If the deployment uses local KMS or VA storage, those pods fail until you complete step 4, so `--wait` cannot succeed: Helm waits for them to become ready, times out, exits with an error and marks the release `failed`. The hooks have already run by then, and every manifest, including the new StatefulSets and their PVCs, has been applied. Helm does not roll anything back without `--atomic`. Continue with step 4, then re-run the same `helm upgrade` command so it completes and Helm records the release as `deployed`. Both hooks are idempotent, so they are safe to run again."],[2139,"If the first CA is deleted, CRL generation for the remaining one fails because the configured signer no longer matches a CA with that name. Until the role’s signer is updated to a certificate of the remaining CA, the last published list keeps being served until it expires (7 days by default)."],[2036,"If the identity stops being trustworthy, Lamassu records the reason and time of revocation. OCSP and CRLs let consumers learn that status."],[2449,"If the key must be generated and kept on the destination system, use **Upload Existing CSR**, described in the authorities guide."],[2247,"If the key will be born inside Lamassu, the recommended flow is to generate a new pair managed directly by one of the configured engines."],[964,"If the previous certificate is ACTIVE, attempts Superseded revocation before binding its successor."],[1132,"If the previous identity is already revoked, retaining its files does not restore a valid connection. Recovery requires an admitted credential and authorized intervention according to the situation."],[1652,"If the result fails"],[2399,"If the result fails"],[2439,"If the result fails"],[516,"If the route declares constraints, it compares request data with the normalized attributes of the same principal contributing the policy; the policy's own `param_constraints` also pin the granted action to literal request values."],[1929,"If the selected identifier cannot be resolved, the operation fails; it does not silently fall back to the CA’s profile. Avoid setting both options at once. Recording which profile was selected helps explain why a certificate differs from its CSR."],[1457,"If the Thing already existed in AWS IoT Core, the connector iterates all certificates (principals) currently attached to that Thing and marks them as `REVOKED` in AWS, ensuring previous certificates cannot keep being used to authenticate."],[822,"If unavailable, **Issue New Instead** starts issuance using the intended CA. Review the profile first."],[277,"If users connect by DNS name, put that name in `tls.certManagerOptions.certSpec.hostnames`. Do not copy an upstream proxy's public IP into `gateway.addresses`."],[1975,"If using both, define when fallback is allowed and what the client does when retrieval, signature or freshness checks fail, or the result is `Unknown`. Accepting an identity when its status cannot be checked must be an explicit application decision."],[1713,"If you are still preparing the instance, start in Deployment. The Platform paths assume an accessible console and permission to perform the operations."],[1689,"If you cannot select the DMS, confirm its creation and your read permissions."],[2451,"If you continue with manual registration, the `Device ID` will be exactly `device-001`."],[1905,"If you enable **Honor Key Usage** or **Honor Extended Key Usages**, Lamassu keeps the values requested by the CSR. If you disable them, it replaces them with the ones defined in the profile."],[2736,"If you kept the 3.8.0 role defaults (`realm_access.roles` and `pki-admin`), the default `services.authz.bootstrap` already matches them."],[724,"If you need retention, search, tamper protection or regulatory export, connect a consumer that persists the events into the system approved by your organization."],[414,"If you receive a 401:"],[1211,"If you receive an identity, check the key, subject, use and connection with the consumer."],[451,"if you set `amqp.ca.secretName`, confirm the Secret exists in the release namespace and its data key matches `amqp.ca.certificateKey` (default `ca.crt`); if `amqp.ca.secretName` is not set, the clients trust the system CAs;"],[2458,"If you use an intermediate CA, the trusted root must be in `ca.pem` and you must supply the intermediates with `-untrusted intermediates.pem`. This variant is covered in the trust model."],[228,"If your values file still carries the legacy gateway JWT validation settings — `auth.oidc.apiGateway.jwks`, `auth.authorization.rolesClaim` or `auth.authorization.roles.admin` — remove them. The migration guide `charts/lamassu/CHANGELOG/3.8.0->4.0.0.md` documents their removal with no compatibility aliases, and `values.schema.json` rejects the leftover blocks instead of silently ignoring them. The replacements are `services.authz.jwkUrl` and `services.authz.bootstrap`, already shown in the example above."],[1001,"Implement renewal and recovery and trust updates. The EST reference covers endpoints, formats and ServerKeyGen; troubleshooting helps distinguish transport, admission and persistence failures."],[2201,"Import an existing pair"],[1813,"Import an external CA"],[2215,"Import keys created externally."],[1857,"Import the certificate and, when applicable, its PEM key. Register the CAs needed to identify the issuer. Registering a CA does not install it in external trust stores."],[2254,"Importing lets you register in Lamassu a key generated outside the platform. It is the usual flow in BYOK scenarios, migrations or integration with cryptographic material already in production."],[2406,"In **Certification Authorities**, select **Create New CA** and choose to create a CA with a new key pair."],[1838,"In **Certification Authorities**, select **Create New CA** and choose to generate a new key pair."],[1662,"In **Enrollment CA**, select the CA that issued `device.crt`."],[1663,"In **Registration Mode**, select `PRE_REGISTRATION` for the path that starts with explicit registration."],[1381,"In `auto` mode: creates/updates policies, ensures Thing groups, revokes stale principals and provisions the Thing via `iot:RegisterThing`. In `jitp` mode: no AWS calls; AWS runs the JITP template on first connection."],[1440,"In `jitp` mode the connector makes no provisioning calls: AWS executes the JITP template on the device's first connection and creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it on its own."],[1375,"In `jitp` mode, attempts to create the provisioning template and attaches it to the registered CA. Non-idempotent: a `PUT /v1/dms/{id}` edit retries the creation and lands in `lamassu-dlq` after 3 retries. Idempotent for `none` and `auto` (configuration only)."],[2684,"In 3.8.0 `amqp.tls` was not applied and event-bus clients always connected over plaintext. In 4.0.0 `amqp.tls: true` switches them to AMQPS. The default `amqp.port` stays `5672` and does not change when TLS is enabled, so a values file that already set `amqp.tls: true` must point `amqp.port` at the broker's TLS listener (`amqps`, commonly `5671`), or set `amqp.tls: false` to keep plaintext. Clients must also trust the broker's certificate."],[2592,"In 3.8.0 the API Gateway validated tokens: a JWT policy on every private route checked the signature and only let through tokens that carried the administrator role. Every route was open to any token that passed that check."],[1046,"In a Bash session with `set -euo pipefail` and `umask 077`, use `EST_BASE` and the trust/bootstrap files from EST client. Select an available and admitted `est-serverkeygen-001`. The `server-request.key` only signs the input CSR; the received identity uses the server-generated key."],[1626,"In a second terminal, enter the same directory and run `umask 077`. Create a local server certificate and start a test consumer. This server trusts `device-root.pem` and requires a valid client identity:"],[760,"In a self-managed deployment, Lamassu uses PostgreSQL for persistence, RabbitMQ for messaging and an OIDC provider for authentication. See the deployment architecture for how the services are published on Kubernetes."],[2460,"In Bash, compare the two public-key digests:"],[1469,"In JITP mode the device also performs its EST enrollment conventionally and obtains its certificate from Lamassu. The difference is that AWS IoT Core does not know the device until it attempts its first connection."],[1625,"In Lamassu, open `est-device-001`. It must be created with the intended owning DMS, a current identity and the received certificate in its history. Compare the local serial with the slot; thresholds can show a renewal state rather than simply Active."],[2072,"In Lamassu, trust does not simply mean that a certificate exists in the inventory. A consumer trusts an identity when it can build a chain to a root it recognizes, check that the certificate is fit for the intended use and verify that it is still valid."],[1035,"In mTLS mode, the enrollment CA always validates the re-enrollment credential; certificates from other issuers are accepted only if they are listed as validation CAs in the re-enrollment block. The expiry check through `enable_expired_renewal` belongs to that validator. A revoked current certificate and a request before the window are rejected. See Renewal for installation and recovery."],[2213,"In practice, it lets you:"],[627,"In the `authz` logs, an external endpoint decision includes `allowed`, `reason`, `matched_principals`, `evaluated_policy_ids`, `matched_policy_id` and the status code. These fields let you separate a matching failure from a permissions failure. With OpenTelemetry instrumentation enabled, each decision also produces a span — `engine.CheckHTTP` on the Gateway path — whose attributes record `authz.allowed`, the matched policy and the matched principal, so you can correlate the denial with the Gateway trace."],[1664,"In the authentication section, enable **Require client certificate** and leave **Call external webhook** off. Under **Require client certificate**, add the CA whose initial certificates you will accept to **Validation CAs**; for this evaluation, it can be the same CA. Keep **Verify CSR Signature** enabled and leave **Enable server-side key generation** off if you will not use it."],[2211,"In the current configuration, Lamassu IoT works exclusively with asymmetric keys, such as RSA and elliptic curves. These keys are used to issue X.509 certificates, sign requests and perform authentication and validation operations within the system."],[366,"In the default `tls.type: certManager` mode, cert-manager `Issuer` and CA `Certificate` resources and a chart-managed downstream `Certificate`; in the `tls.type: external` mode, a reference to your TLS Secret"],[2079,"In the device-generated CSR flow, the CA applies the profile and signs without receiving the device’s private key."],[1240,"In the DMS, separate distributed CAs from the re-enrollment validation CAs at `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`, used for re-enrollment credentials. That list is optional because the enrollment CA always validates re-enrollment credentials, but when switching the enrollment CA, add the previous issuance CA to it so old-CA identities keep re-enrolling during overlap. The inspected validator tries the enrollment CA followed by those CAs against the received leaf; accepting a multilevel chain requires checking what reaches the service and which issuer is configured."],[1097,"In the inspected code, finding any device owned by the same DMS triggers the `enable_replaceable_enrollment` check before PRE\\_REGISTRATION. With the flag disabled, a preregistered device can receive `forbiddenNewEnrollment` even in **No Identity**. Selecting PRE\\_REGISTRATION alone therefore does not establish a working path."],[1501,"In the policy above, the `<AWS_REGION>` and `<AWS_ACCOUNT_ID>` placeholders must be replaced with the AWS region and the twelve-digit account identifier of the target account before applying the policy. The `${iot:Connection.Thing.ThingName}` variable automatically limits each permission to the Thing the device is connected as, following the principle of least privilege. The `${ShadowName}` variable in the shadow ARNs is a placeholder that must be replaced with the `shadow_config.shadow_name` value configured in the DMS; if the Classic Shadow is used, it can be simplified by removing the `name/${ShadowName}/` segment. AWS IoT policies do not support regex alternation; the `[name/${ShadowName}/]*` notation represents the optionality of that segment within ARN syntax constraints."],[2258,"In the wizard, choose **Import Existing Key Pair** and complete the requested fields:"],[2446,"In this flow, the client's key is generated in the browser and must be downloaded when issuance finishes. If you lose it, you must generate a key and issue another certificate. The certificate inventory lets you download the public certificate, but cannot recover this key."],[1453,"In this mode the connector **creates no Things, groups or policies** in AWS IoT Core. It only syncs certificate status (active, revoked, suspended) as it changes in Lamassu. It is the right mode when Thing provisioning in AWS is managed by an external system and Lamassu only needs to keep certificate status up to date."],[436,"inaccessible image or incorrect pull policy;"],[1617,"Include Enrollment CA enabled; Include Lamassu System CA disabled."],[480,"Included policies"],[297,"Independent Gateways and separate external IPs"],[300,"Independent Gateways on one node and one IP"],[2569,"Independent re-enrollment authentication"],[2673,"Independent re-enrollment authentication"],[1749,"Infrastructure operator"],[4,"Infrastructure requirements"],[1602,"Initial authentication"],[1028,"Initial authentication and admission; server generation enabled."],[1022,"Initial authentication and DMS registration/replacement policy."],[947,"Initial authentication and renewal settings are stored independently. If a DMS only has the initial authentication block, renewal reuses it: read both blocks before editing. The console exposes independent editors for the enrollment and re-enrollment authentication, with the same Log Level, Call Timeout, HTTP Authentication and cred