ll 30-second request timeout bounds each call. If an egress proxy handles the cluster's outbound traffic, check the pod's `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables and list in-cluster service names in `NO_PROXY`."],[318,"Internal Lamassu clients — the Vault engine connection, service-to-service calls and webhook deliveries — honor the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables. The exception is the authorization-engine connector: it uses its own transport and does not consult these proxy variables. If your deployment routes egress through a proxy, list in-cluster service names in `NO_PROXY` (for example `.svc,.cluster.local`) so internal traffic stays inside the cluster. Loopback traffic is never sent through a proxy."],[437,"invalid cryptographic engine configuration;"],[1637,"Inventory"],[1758,"Inventory the dependencies"],[1527,"IoT Fleets"],[1534,"IoT Fleets applies PKI to the device lifecycle: registration, obtaining an identity, renewal and retirement. It is intended for those who administer a device population and those who integrate its enrollment client or the system that accepts it."],[1722,"IoT Fleets overview"],[2023,"Issuance"],[751,"Issuance and enrollment"],[2511,"Issuance fails"],[871,"Issuance profile"],[1594,"Issuance profile"],[1925,"Issuance through a DMS"],[2020,"Issuance, use, renewal, revocation and retirement of certificates in Lamassu."],[1586,"Issue a bootstrap leaf certificate with `CN=est-device-001`, its own key and Client Authentication use. Follow Issue and verify a certificate, changing its CN and saving the result as `bootstrap.crt` and `bootstrap.key`. Choose validity that covers this test and check the pair and chain."],[2428,"Issue a client certificate and check content, chain and key correspondence."],[1704,"Issue and control certificates"],[2364,"Issue and control certificates"],[2434,"Issue and download"],[2427,"Issue and verify a certificate"],[1819,"Issue certificates"],[1989,"Issue from an existing CSR"],[2097,"Issue from intermediate CAs separated by environment or risk domain."],[2469,"Issuer cannot be found"],[1748,"Issuer trust, certificate validation and permissions in the destination."],[2031,"Issuing a certificate is only the beginning. During its useful life you must check its validity, renew it before it expires and retire it when it stops being trustworthy. Lamassu keeps the certificate, its issuing CA, its status and, when it belongs to a device, the identity it is linked to."],[1593,"Issuing root from `device-root.pem`."],[825,"Issuing, binding and installing are separate steps. If an operation fails, check which steps persisted before repeating it."],[1445,"It calls `iot:GetRegistrationCode` to obtain AWS's verification code."],[744,"it can be retrieved by resource, actor and period;"],[2502,"It identifies `device-001`, supports client authentication and verifies against `ca.pem`."],[2500,"It is a CA certificate, is currently valid and its self-signature verifies."],[1447,"It sends the CA (in PEM) and the verification certificate to AWS via `iot:RegisterCACertificate`, enabling registration with `AllowAutoRegistration: true`."],[513,"It separately loads the policies granted to each principal."],[2383,"its chain reaches a root trusted by the consumer;"],[2384,"its dates and usages allow the operation;"],[2443,"Its public certificate in `ca.pem`; this example uses the root created in the previous path."],[2382,"its public key matches the client's private key;"],[2504,"Its public key matches the one included in `device.crt`."],[322,"Its status contains the expected address."],[2381,"its subject and extensions identify the expected client;"],[25,"Just want to evaluate the platform?"],[1461,"Just-In-Time Provisioning (JITP) mode delegates provisioning to AWS instead of the connector doing it directly. When the connector detects the DMS is in `jitp` mode, it creates a JITP provisioning template in AWS IoT Core and attaches it to the registered CA certificate. The connector attempts to create this template when the DMS is created and again on every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) — not at device enrollment time. The attempt is not idempotent: when a template named after the DMS ID already exists, the creation call fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in the `lamassu-dlq` poison queue (see Troubleshooting). The template is created once; later DMS edits never update its content. A DMS update that omits `metadata` or sends `null` keeps the stored metadata, so the connector key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}` remains present and the attempt is still triggered. A body that supplies a `metadata` object replaces the stored metadata entirely: if that object does not carry the connector key, the key is dropped and the connector silently skips the DMS's future events. Once the template is attached, when a device with a certificate issued by that CA connects to AWS IoT Core for the first time, AWS executes the template automatically: it creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it."],[259,"K3s"],[280,"K3s ServiceLB implements each `LoadBalancer` Service with pods that reserve the Service ports on the node. On a single node, only one Service can therefore own port 80 and one can own port 443. The default K3s Traefik Service may already hold both ports."],[611,"Keep `failOpen: false` unless a documented risk assessment justifies otherwise."],[894,"Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession."],[613,"Keep at least two recovery administrators and test their access periodically."],[2100,"Keep enough overlap to renew a disconnected fleet."],[1233,"Keep initial EST server trust separate from identity consumer trust."],[727,"keep the complete CloudEvent unmodified;"],[1678,"Keep the device identifier, owning DMS and serial number as references for the result."],[1130,"Keep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinate